From 872b56664dad3da5af2369ea9527293123c38907 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:53:58 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 360 viewer: Xbox 360 gamepad fallback for
 pause/recenter/next/prev/quit

Requested so the player has a controls path that doesn't depend on a paired VR
controller. This rig's own known gap: Monado never re-probes for controllers after
session startup, so a session started with the WMR controllers off/unpaired - or
that loses them mid-run - has no controller input at all until the whole session
restarts. A USB Xbox 360 pad is already this rig's no-controllers-needed fallback
for Aircar; this gives the 360/VR180 player the same option.

New gamepadinput.h/.cpp is a third, fully independent producer into the exact same
PlayerControl:: functions the OpenXR controller-action poll (openxr_program.cpp)
and the raw-stdin keyboard thread (main.cpp) already both call - no control logic
duplicated, just a second input source read on its own detached thread, the same
architecture patch 0003's keyboard thread already established.

Reads the pad directly as a Linux joystick device (/dev/input/jsN, the joydev API)
rather than going through OpenXR/Monado - a real USB Xbox 360 pad is not visible to
Monado on this rig at all (no prober/driver ever instantiates an xrt_device for it,
confirmed by grepping the whole drivers/ tree; binding an action to
/interaction_profiles/microsoft/xbox_controller would pass validation but resolve
to a permanently unbound, always-isActive=false action). Located via
/sys/class/input/jsN/device/id/{vendor,product} for the same 045e:028e VID:PID this
repo's own scripts already use to detect an Xbox 360 pad (scripts/power-on.py,
scripts/reseat_audio.py) - never a hardcoded jsN path, which the kernel is free to
reassign across replugs/reboots/other joysticks.

Mapped: A pause/resume, Start recenter, RB next track, LB previous track, hold
Guide ~1.5s to quit (mirrors the VR controller's Menu-hold gesture - hold, don't
tap; releasing early cancels with no cooldown). joydev button events are already
edges (one event per press, one per release), so unlike the VR controller's analog
trigger/thumbstick there is no hysteresis-latch to reimplement; each press calls
its PlayerControl:: function exactly once. B/X/Y/Select/both thumbstick clicks are
read (harmlessly ignored) but not bound to anything, and the sticks/triggers
aren't read at all yet - kept to the requested core-controls scope, both are a
natural follow-up.

Genuinely hotplug-capable in both directions, unlike the VR controller path: the
kernel creates/destroys the jsN node as the pad is plugged/unplugged, and the
thread just keeps polling for it (open() failures and mid-session read/poll
errors alike just go back to searching, logged, never fatal), so a pad connected
mid-session or unplugged and replugged works with no restart.

Build-verified clean (`ninja hello_xr`, -Wall plus this project's usual
-Werror=implicit-fallthrough/-Werror=undef/-Werror=missing-braces/
-Werror=unreachable-code, no warnings). This rig had a real Xbox 360 pad plugged in
(Bus 003 Device 003: ID 045e:028e) during this change, so beyond the build a
standalone host-side harness was compiled and linked against the actual compiled
gamepadinput.cpp/playercontrol.cpp/logger.cpp objects (the same ones hello_xr
itself links) and run entirely outside OpenXR/Vulkan/Monado: idle for several
seconds first (found and opened /dev/input/js0, zero spurious PlayerControl state
changes), then again with scripts/usb-reset-device.py firing a real USB reset on
the pad mid-run to force a genuine disconnect/reconnect. Result: "disconnected"
logged within one poll cycle of the reset, "connected (/dev/input/js0)" logged
~1s later once the kernel re-enumerated it, no crash or hang on either side of the
cycle - directly exercising the "must not crash/hang if unplugged mid-session"
requirement this was built to.

NOT verified: no button was actually pressed (no hands on this SSH session), so
the button-index-to-PlayerControl:: dispatch itself is checked only against
`jstest --normal`'s live report against the real pad ("11 buttons (BtnA, BtnB,
BtnX, BtnY, BtnTL, BtnTR, BtnSelect, BtnStart, BtnMode, BtnThumbL, BtnThumbR)",
confirming the index order this code assumes), not by pressing one and watching
the player react. Also unverified: coexistence with a live OpenXR/Vulkan render
loop and a real VR controller session running at the same time. Per this
project's own rule, that needs a person wearing the headset with the pad in hand,
not an agent.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013wcJ6UeY8LBrZJu9hwH8vB
